INT. CHURCH BELL TOWER, GHOST TOWN - DAWN

Dust in slanting light. A rope hangs slack. Four people share a space built for one.

JEB TALLMAN, 50s, bandaged ribs, sits against the wall with a Colt on his knee, while CORA LEE PRUETT, 30s, in a torn calico dress, peels back his shirt to check the wound, and PIKE, 20s, cheeks smeared with soot, keeps watch at the slit window, and DOC AMBROSE, 60s, spectacles cracked, tips the last of his flask over Jeb's side.

JEB
It's quiet, Pike.

PIKE
Quiet's worse, Jeb.

CORA LEE
Quiet means they ran out of whiskey. Or men.

DOC AMBROSE
Or patience, Miss Cora Lee.

JEB
They've got plenty of that. Always did.

PIKE
Doc, how long till he can ride?

DOC AMBROSE
Days. Weeks, if he's sensible.

JEB
I'm never sensible.

CORA LEE
That's why you're here, Jeb Tallman.

Jeb smiles. Lets the Colt slide off his knee. Pike turns from the window, rifle loose in his hand.

PIKE
They're gone.
